  'init.error.session-unavailable': 'The session instance is unavailable',
  'init.prompt': 'Generate (or update) the AGENTS.md file at the workspace root.\n\nWorkspace root: {root}\n\nRequirements:\n1. First analyze the project background: the README, build and dependency manifests (e.g. package.json / pyproject.toml / Cargo.toml / go.mod), source layout, and primary languages.\n2. Generate AGENTS.md at the root, covering: project overview, common commands (build / test / check / run), tech stack, directory structure, and coding and collaboration conventions.\n3. Keep AGENTS.md concise and accurate, containing only what is necessary; do not put complex logic (such as architecture design, data flows, module details, incident postmortems) into AGENTS.md itself — instead create a docs folder, organize that complex logic into Markdown documents inside it, and reference them from AGENTS.md.\n4. If AGENTS.md already exists: keep the valuable content and merge updates on top of it; do not blindly overwrite.\n5. Write the document in the same language as the project\'s existing docs (Chinese for Chinese projects, English for English ones).\n6. Briefly summarize what you generated or updated when done.',
  'init.prompt.audit': 'Map the functional interfaces of the current workspace to their implementations, produce a document, and audit the overall architecture.\n\nWorkspace root: {root}\n\nScope: read-only analysis - do not change any business code; the deliverable is a document, not a refactor.\n\nDeliverable: write docs/interface-audit.md under {root} (create the folder if missing):\n1. Interface-to-implementation map: list every exposed functional interface (commands, HTTP endpoints, exported APIs, UI entry points, shortcuts) and, for each one, the single place that implements it (file:symbol).\n2. Redundancy list: for an interface or feature with several implementations, several styles, or several entry points, list those locations and say which one should stay and which should be deleted or merged.\n3. Architecture review: modules placed at the wrong layer, the same data carried back and forth between layers, and the redundant interfaces and performance cost that causes; every item needs its data path (A -> B -> C) and a direction for the fix.\n4. Order the findings by impact and keep only what is worth fixing; do not pad the document with aspects that are fine.\n\nCriteria (first principles):\n- Judge the existing structure by what the feature minimally needs, not by justifying the abstractions that are already there.\n- No fallback branches for hypothetical scenarios, no redundant wrappers.\n- If two pieces of code solve it, do not split it into four layers of abstraction.\n\nWrite the document in the same language as the project\'s existing docs; finish with a brief summary of what you produced and the findings most worth fixing.',
  'init.prompt.cleanup': 'Trim the current workspace: remove everything that is already dead and compress valid but verbose comments to their shortest form - subtraction only.\n\nWorkspace root: {root}\n\nScope: documents in the repository (the docs folder and scattered .md / .txt), prompt and template files, HTML prototypes and one-off pages, and source-code comments; nothing outside these four classes.\n\nOnly two kinds of change are allowed: deletion (files, document sections, comments) and comment compression (rewriting a valid but verbose comment shorter). Do not add files or documents, do not rewrite documents or code bodies, do not change any code logic or behavior.\n\nDeletion criteria (every item must point at evidence; without evidence it goes to "needs confirmation"):\n1. Outdated document content: the interfaces, paths, config keys, fields or commands it describes no longer exist or were renamed - name the symbol and where it really lives today, or state that it no longer exists.\n2. Resolved temporary artifacts: fix reports, incident postmortems, design drafts, one-off audit reports, migration notes - delete the ones whose conclusions already live in the README / AGENTS.md / spec documents; do not write the conclusions for the ones that have not landed, put those under "needs confirmation".\n3. Dangling references: files, symbols, routes or commands referenced by documents, comments or directories that no longer exist.\n4. Useless comments: ones that contradict the code, describe a deleted implementation, remain from a version migration, restate the code line by line, or are commented-out dead code - version control is the backup, delete them.\n5. Duplicates and shells: duplicate documents wholly covered by another one, empty directories, and documents with only a title or a "to be written" placeholder.\n\nCompression criteria (comments only; follow every item):\n6. Keep the reason, drop the restatement: sentences carrying constraints, boundaries, pitfalls, design rationale or external references are real information and must stay; lines that restate the code or narrate the obvious get cut.\n7. If one sentence says it, do not spend three; keep each fact in one place and merge repeats into a single sentence.\n8. Compression changes word count only, never facts: meaning, constraints and conditions must not be lost or altered; keep the comment language consistent with the other comments in the file and preserve the existing comment style and formatting (such as block-comment wrapping and alignment).\n9. When unsure whether a sentence should go, keep it as written; if the rewrite is not shorter or clearer than the original, restore the original.\n\nKeep (do not touch): live documents such as README, AGENTS.md, LICENSE and CHANGELOG; build, dependency and test configuration; lockfiles; spec and red-line documents; anything still referenced by source, the build, tests or other documents (including fixtures and samples in use); dependency folders and build output; and the constraints, pitfalls and design rationale carried by valid comments (compression may reword them, never drop them).\n\nBefore deleting each item, check: what it is; why it is stale (evidence); which search terms proved nothing references it and where the hits were. If a single hit is not the item itself, keep it.\n\nIf the workspace is not under version control: delete nothing and only produce the list; compression may proceed, but report the pre-compression text for every comment you shorten.\n\nWhen done: run the build / check / test commands the workspace provides and confirm they still pass; report in four blocks - (1) deleted (path -> one-line reason), (2) compressed (path:location -> one line on what was cut), (3) needs confirmation (why it is uncertain), (4) key items explicitly kept. Do not write a summary document; the change itself is the record.',
